rm that partly resembles  the english one  star is like the est ending in english  but this one might not be the best  example come to think of it because it's  irregular most single syllable  adjectives take the ending  most expensive the form with the e at  the end  aste is used when the adjective is  definite  particularly when preceded by a definite  article or genitive pronoun  multi-syllable adjectives form the  superlative when mest  comes before them like english most most  intelligent  would be mist intelligent the rules  about single-syllable words and  multi-syllable words are not 100  in fact this word could also be said  with ast rather than  messed but the rule is generally true  swedish stored in stockholm the biggest  city in sweden is stockholm  word for word sweden's biggest city is  stockholm  stockholm gothenburg isn't as big as  stockholm  word for word it's gothenburg is not  like  big as stockholm comparisons of equality  are formed in swedish by using  plus an adjective followed by some  literally  equal adjective as  is a cognate of english like in its use  as an adverb  you are as tall as ella word for word  you are equally tall as ella  you may be getting the sense that  swedish is quite similar to english in  its grammar and to some extent in its  germanic vocabulary  english-speaking learners of swedish are  often struck by just how similar the two  are  and by how intuitive swedish is for them  the bigger challenges in learning  swedish seem to lie in the pronunciation  in the early stages and in getting used  to dialectal variation and different  accents in the later stages  but all in all people who learn swedish  overwhelmingly enjoy the process  and their efforts are well rewarded the  question of the day  for speakers of swedish to what extent  would you say that you speak  standard swedish and to what extent do  you use your local dialect  and to people who have studied swedish  what has your experience been like  what have you fo